<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amy Ashwood Garvey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1897">1897</a>–<a href="/years/1969">1969</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Amy Ashwood, Akosua Boahemaa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amy Ashwood Garvey was a Jamaican Pan-Africanist activist, born in Port Antonio, who with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-garvey">Marcus Garvey</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-negro-improvement-association">Universal Negro Improvement Association</a> (UNIA) in 1914 and organized its women's section. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> she was secretary of the UNIA's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> branch and in 1919 became one of the first directors of the Black Star Line; she married Garvey that year, and he divorced her in 1922. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> she opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> Mills Social Club with Sam Manning in 1934, and in 1945 she helped organize the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifth-pan-african-congress">5th Pan-African Congress</a> in Manchester. After the 1958 Notting Hill race riots she co-founded the Association for the Advancement of Coloured People.</p></div>
